<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyrrhic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">280–275 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pyrrhic War was fought from 280 to 275 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Pyrrhus, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to help the Greek city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>. Pyrrhus defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at Heraclea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> but with heavy losses, giving rise to the phrase Pyrrhic victory. After a campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> he was checked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, leaving Rome master of southern Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrrhic-war.jpg" alt="Pyrrhic War" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Megistias · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Pyrrhic_War_(280%E2%80%93275_BC)_(English).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
